{
  "dom_id": "step-q108838",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2y)}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=C-2x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z & =x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡z & =C-2x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)=C-2x\\,\\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
